# Manifest gates every storage write (R2)

`pim-app-config` is the single abstraction over per-binary settings
storage (registry on Windows, `/etc/aic/<program>/settings.json` on
Linux, plist on macOS, OS keyring for secrets). Every program ships a
static `app-config.toml` enumerating the keys it understands, their
types, defaults, secret flags, and validation rules — so configurator
GUIs can render typed widgets without hard-coding a schema, the CLI can
refuse `set FOO=…` for keys the daemon never reads, and orphans from old
releases can be enumerated and purged on upgrade.

The audit found `set_value` / `unset_value` inconsistently refusing
unknown keys: an operator could `set TYPO_KEY=value`, see "ok", and
never learn the daemon ignored it.

## The rule

> **`pim_app_config::set_value` and `pim_app_config::unset_value` MUST
> refuse a key that is not declared in the active manifest, returning a
> typed `AppConfigError::UnknownKey { key, manifest }`. CLI `set` /
> `unset` subcommands surface that error with a non-zero exit code and a
> US-English diagnostic naming the manifest. The `orphans` subcommand
> (or its per-binary embedded equivalent) lists / purges keys present in
> storage but absent from the manifest.**

`get_value` is unaffected — reads stay permissive so an older daemon can
load a newer settings file without crashing.

## Required library surface

```rust
pub fn set(&self, key: &str, value: &str) -> Result<(), AppConfigError> {
    let entry = self.manifest.entry(key).ok_or_else(|| AppConfigError::UnknownKey {
        key: key.to_string(),
        manifest: self.manifest.program_id().to_string(),
    })?;
    entry.validate(value).map_err(|reason| AppConfigError::InvalidValue {
        key: key.to_string(), reason,
        manifest: self.manifest.program_id().to_string(),
    })?;
    self.backend.put(key, value, entry.is_secret())?;
    Ok(())
}
```

`unset_value` performs the same `manifest.entry(key).ok_or_else(…)?`
check before `backend.remove(key)`.

## CLI exit codes (pinned for SIEM / scripting)

| Code | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `0` | Success |
| `2` | `UnknownKey` — manifest does not declare the requested key |
| `3` | `InvalidValue` — declared, but the value fails validation |
| `4` | `Storage` — backend read/write failure (registry locked, permissions, keyring offline) |
| `64..=78` | Reserved per `sysexits.h` for CLI-argument usage / config / data errors |

Stdout is empty on error; stderr carries the operator-facing US-English
message. Never `println!("ok")` after a write that returned `Err`.

## Orphan purge subcommand

```text
pim-app-config orphans [--program <id>] [--purge] [--yes] [--json]

  Lists keys present in the backend for <program> that the active
  manifest does not declare. --purge removes each after confirmation
  (skipped with --yes). --json emits machine-readable output for
  upgrade scripts.

Exit codes:
  0  no orphans found (or all purged)
  5  orphans found, no --purge requested (informational)
  6  --purge requested but at least one removal failed
```

Per-binary configurators embed this under the same name so an MSI
upgrade script need not depend on a separate `pim-app-config` binary.
Secret-marked orphans are purged from the secret backend and reported as
`[secret len=N] (purged)` — never echoing the value.

## Forbidden constructs

- `self.backend.put(key, value)` without a prior
  `self.manifest.entry(key).ok_or_else(…)?`. Always go through
  `AppConfig::set_value`.
- `let _ = config.set_value(key, value);` — discarding the `Result`
  discards the rejection that is the whole point of the rule.
- `println!("ok")` / `dialog_show_success(...)` on a path that has not
  matched on the `Result` first.
- A `set` / `unset` CLI subcommand returning exit code 0 on
  `UnknownKey` — the calling script cannot tell success from a typo.
- A configurator "Save" that catches `UnknownKey` and silently drops it.
  Render it in the status pane, keep the field highlighted, do not close
  the dialog.

## Worked violation → fix

**Violation:** `fn cmd_set(...) { let _ = config.backend.put(key, value,
false); println!("ok"); }` — a typo reports success and the daemon never
reads the key.

**Fix:** call `config.set_value(key, value)` and match every variant,
returning `ExitCode::from(2)` for `UnknownKey` with `error: unknown
configuration key '<key>' for program '<manifest>'`, `3` for
`InvalidValue`, `4` for `Storage`. The configurator GUI calls the same
`set_value` and surfaces the same variants in its status pane.

